\name{lavaangui-multiple-groups}
\alias{lavaangui-multiple-groups}
\title{Multiple-Group Analysis and Measurement Invariance}
\description{The Multiple Groups module refits a common specification across a separate grouping variable. It is not a multiple-group editor or an importer of already fitted multiple-group lavaan objects.}
\details{
Estimate a diagram with the current data, open More Results, choose Multiple Groups, and select Grouping column. The column must be separate from the modeled variables and have 2--10 distinct nonblank values. Missing or blank group values are excluded; group labels are ordered by first occurrence and the first is the reference. Each group needs at least two rows before fitting; this is only a technical guard, not a sample-size recommendation. Read the actual used counts per stage in Fit table.

Select one of the following workflows, set its options, check I have checked the grouping variable, measurement type and assumptions last, and click Run multigroup analysis. Changing options requires a new confirmation. Results are organized in Fit table, Selected estimates, Path diagram, Advanced diagnostics and Export & restore.
}
\section{Continuous measurement invariance}{
Measurement invariance (CFA) uses continuous ML/MLR, first-marker scaling, automatic variances and a mean structure. Do not use structural regressions in this sequence. It fits configural (common pattern), metric (equal loadings), scalar (equal loadings and intercepts) and optionally strict (also equal residual variances). The differences are sequential; interpret later tests conditional on previous stages. Configural fit, identification and measurement theory must be defensible before interpreting equality. Latent means may be freed outside the reference group by the scalar constraints.

To test partial invariance, check Specify exceptions (partial invariance), select Loadings free across groups and/or Intercepts free across groups, and re-confirm. Releases apply to relevant stages and remain in subsequent stages. Marker loadings cannot be released. Partial invariance requires simple structure and at least two indicators per factor whose loadings and intercepts both remain invariant. Residual-variance releases are not available; strict testing adds equality of residual variances. Releases are exploratory unless justified independently. The workflow excludes higher-order factors, labeled parameters, explicit intercept syntax, defined effects, special constraints and additional fixed variances or nonzero coefficients. Explicit zero paths or covariances may be supported. Continuous model columns must be numeric and finite or missing.
}
\section{Ordinal measurement invariance}{
Choose Ordinal measurement invariance (WLSMV) for simple correlated-factor CFA with every indicator declared ordered, at least three indicators per factor, and 4--12 observed categories per indicator after listwise deletion. Category sets must match across groups, with at least five complete observations per category per group. This guard is not a guarantee of adequate information. Enable automatic variances and latent covariances. No cross-loadings, correlated indicator residuals, higher-order factors, mixed continuous-ordinal indicators, binary or three-category indicators, structural regressions, partial ordinal invariance, special labels or additional fixed parameters are supported in this prototype.

The optional semTools package generates syntax with \code{measEq.syntax}, \code{ID.fac = "std.lv"}, \code{ID.cat = "Wu.Estabrook.2016"}, \code{parameterization = "theta"} and WLSMV. The stages are Configural, Thresholds, Metric, Scalar and optional Strict. They constrain thresholds, then loadings, then intercepts of underlying responses, then their residual variances. Scale-identifying constraints change across stages. Use the generated syntax and actual test options rather than treating ordinal models as continuous. The exported R script retains the generated syntax. Compare using corrected scaled-shifted tests and inspect missing robust measures as unavailable, not zero.
}
\section{Equal regressions}{
Choose Equal regressions (path model / SEM). The reference fit has free structural regressions; the other fit constrains all free regressions or only the selected paths, leaving other regressions free. For latent models, justify the common measurement scale and choose equal loadings (metric) or equal loadings and intercepts (scalar); the same measurement restrictions are used in both structural fits. The measurement choice is ignored for observed path models. Equality tests concern unstandardized coefficients. A joint test of several paths does not identify which individual coefficient differs. Targeted tests selected after looking at results are exploratory. The supported scope is continuous ML/MLR with the same restrictions on model syntax as above.
}
\section{Examples}{The installed kit includes 01_AFC_continue.lvm (grouping column groupe; releases f1 =~ x3 and y3 ~ 1), 07_Ordinale_B_WLSMV.lvm (all six indicators ordered; five-stage sequence), 02_Chemins_A_ML.lvm (compare all or selected regressions M ~ Z, Y ~ M), and 08_SEM_latent_ML.lvm (latent direct regression). See \code{\link{lavaangui-advanced}} for access to the kit.}
\references{Wu, H. and Estabrook, R. (2016). Identification of Confirmatory Factor Analysis Models of Different Levels of Invariance for Ordered Categorical Outcomes. \emph{Psychometrika}, 81, 1014--1045. \doi{10.1007/s11336-016-9506-0}. See also \url{https://search.r-project.org/CRAN/refmans/semTools/html/measEq.syntax.html}.}
